<BaseLayout title="ILMP | Intelligent Layered Media Packing" description="ILMP is an experimental media-format ecosystem developed with Lattice Forge.">
<section class="shell hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">Lattice Forge / ILMP</p><h1>Media formats treated as a family, not a single compromise.</h1></div><p class="lede">ILMP, Intelligent Layered Media Packing, is an experimental media-format ecosystem being developed around separate image, animation, and video models with a shared container foundation.</p></section>
<section class="section" id="models"><div class="shell"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Model families</p><h2>Different temporal problems deserve different machinery.</h2></div><div class="grid-3"><article class="card"><div><span class="pill">IMAG</span><h3 style="margin-top:1rem">ILMP Image</h3></div><p>Still-image research built around adaptive regional decisions, progressive layers, and model competition rather than one fixed coding path.</p></article><article class="card"><div><span class="pill">ANIM</span><h3 style="margin-top:1rem">ILMP Animate</h3></div><p>Animation research centered on persistent backgrounds and explicit temporal events such as references, patches, movement, palette changes, and alpha changes.</p></article><article class="card"><div><span class="pill">VIDE</span><h3 style="margin-top:1rem">ILMP Video</h3></div><p>Video research with explicit picture/reference behavior, motion-aware regions, preserved presentation timing, and high-frame-rate correctness as a first-class requirement.</p></article></div></div></section>
<section class="dark-band" id="research"><div class="shell"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Research direction</p><h2>Compression claims have to survive matched-quality measurement.</h2></div><p class="lede muted">ILMP development uses benchmark-oriented iteration across image quality, temporal behavior, file size, and decoder correctness. This site deliberately avoids publishing unsupported ratio victories or benchmark claims.</p></div></section>
<section class="section" id="status"><div class="shell grid-3"><article class="card"><h3>Container 2.0</h3><p>A track-and-chunk container architecture designed to support multiple model families while keeping legacy generations isolated.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>High-FPS video</h3><p>Presentation timing is preserved independently from decode/reference state, with regression goals spanning 24 through 240 FPS.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Release discipline</h3><p>Public release and benchmark sections only become populated when corresponding source data and release artifacts are available.</p></article></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
